I’ll first inventory the measurement, chemical context, available reference-search/refinement helpers, and any existing workspace artifacts. Then I’ll identify candidate phases from peak positions and chemistry, build a physically screened multiphase model, refine it in GSAS-II with residual-led challenges, and export every requested artifact from the final replayed project.

The context points to the Cu–W–O system after a 550 °C reaction, so CuWO₄ is an obvious hypothesis, but I’m treating CuO, WO₃ polymorphs, copper-rich tungstates, and weak residual reagents as live alternatives. I’m now extracting the experimental peak inventory and screening all visible Cu/W/O database records rather than assuming a nominal reaction product.

Two phase families now have coherent, repeated support across the scan: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the diagnostic 23.2/23.7/24.5° triplet and many split families, while cubic Pa‑3 Cu₃WO₆ accounts for independent peaks at about 15.76, 20.37, 33.08, 34.31, 36.81, 58.19, 63.88, 70.80, and 87.65°. I’m checking source conditions and geometry for alternate WO₃ records and keeping CuO/CuWO₄ on the watch list for a common-profile challenge.

The initial two-phase Rietveld model is chemically and crystallographically coherent, with provisional modeled-crystalline fractions near 35 wt% Cu₃WO₆ / 65 wt% WO₃ before profile optimization. Peak positions refine to sensible contracted/expanded cells, but the default instrumental width is much too narrow; I’m now replacing it with a common nonnegative U/W/X baseline and judging widths across isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle families before testing phase-specific size/strain.

The common-profile source comparison is still running for several WO₃ polymorphs. One high-temperature P2₁/m record failed immediately from an invalid imported model/profile combination and will not be treated as evidence against WO₃ itself; the room-temperature monoclinic, neutron monoclinic, triclinic, and orthorhombic alternatives are continuing independently.

The source challenge favors the room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ model: Rwp ≈18.846% versus 18.964% for the independent neutron monoclinic record, ≈18.97% for the triclinic model (also replay-unstable near higher symmetry), 24.60% orthorhombic, and 30.00% tetragonal. I’m retaining the room-temperature P2₁/n source and now re-challenging CuO and both CuWO₄ records by scale first, with the established common profile held intact.

CuO survives the watch-list challenge with coherent scale support and a plausible refined tenorite cell, improving Rwp from 18.846% to 18.413% and refining to about 8.7 wt% before broadening-model cleanup. Both CuWO₄ structures collapse to the scale floor. I also found that GSAS-II’s nominal “fixed” defaults still carry finite 1 µm size and 1000 µstrain contributions; I’m explicitly neutralizing those hidden terms now, then rebuilding the common profile before any size/strain comparisons.

The cleaned common profile supports a positive U term with X (U≈34.6, W fixed at a small positive floor, X≈15.4); freeing W drives it negative and is rejected, while SH/L gives only a tiny gain and is 97.5% correlated with zero, so it is also rejected. The phase-by-phase size/strain trials are now running from that exact checkpoint; Cu₃WO₆ isotropic strain is provisionally supported (~746 µstrain), while its size and combined trials are taking longer to stabilize.

The smooth six-term background is physically plausible (about 2480 counts at 10°, falling smoothly to ~803 near 70°, then ~856 at 100°), and the low-angle non-peak residual is small; roughness is therefore unsupported. Refining polarization moves only from 0.500 to 0.489 for a negligible 0.0008-point Rwp gain, so it remains fixed. The dominant remaining residual is the WO₃ 00l intensity family, so I’m now testing symmetry-distinct texture axes against axial/diagonal alternatives.

WO₃ texture is unambiguous: a March–Dollase [001] axis lowers Rwp from 18.380% to 4.970% with a stable ratio of 0.7216, far outperforming [100] (15.59%), [010] (18.00%), [101] (18.34%), [110] (8.85%), and [011] (16.90%, unstable). This is supported by the independent 002, 004, and higher 00l families, not a single overlap. I’m retaining [001] and testing Cu₃WO₆/CuO texture only on top of that accepted model.

After the accepted WO₃ texture, the broadening terms remain genuinely complementary: removing Cu₃WO₆ size raises Rwp from 4.932% to 5.222%, removing CuO strain raises it to 5.029%, and removing both raises it to 5.307%. CuO [101] texture gives a smaller but repeatable gain (to 4.932%) across several independent CuO families; Cu₃WO₆ texture is numerically unstable around a neutral ratio and provides no replay-stable gain, so it is omitted. I’m doing the required clean-profile watch-list replay before freezing the provisional final project.

The clean replay rejects CuWO₄: the ambient triclinic candidate reaches only 0.18(8) wt% with a 0.0015-point Rwp gain, and its strongest predicted families mostly worsen rather than improve local residuals; the P2/c candidate is 0.03(6) wt%. I’m excluding both. The provisional final inventory is therefore Cu₃WO₆ + monoclinic WO₃ + CuO, and I’m now saving/reopening that exact project before the mandated staged heavy-site coordinate challenge.

The staged coordinate helper is finding real heavy-site information: WO₃ W2 was accepted after a large Rwp improvement and safe geometry; W1 is being challenged with the helper’s movement/contact guards, and the Cu₃WO₆/CuO heavy sites are still in progress. I’ll adopt the coordinated project only if the helper’s final summary reports accepted sites and the reopened replay preserves the gain.

The helper accepted four heavy sites—WO₃ W2 and W1, plus Cu₃WO₆ Cu1 and W1—with individual Rwp gains of 0.334, 0.026, 0.039, and 0.062 percentage points. All moves were ≤0.056 Å, no variables froze, and contact checks passed; CuO Cu1 was correctly rejected for a negligible 0.00001-point gain. The coordinated project reaches Rwp 4.47077%. I’m performing one independent reopen/replay on the unchanged 8301-point grid, then will adopt and export only from that exact project.

All requested exports now come from the reopened, replayed `final.gpx`. I’m doing a last consistency audit—exact observation-grid preservation, profile-column equality to the project, fraction normalization, CIF readability, and final geometry—without changing the refinement.

Completed the refinement. The modeled crystalline composition is:

| Phase | Space group | Weight fraction |
|---|---|---:|
| WO₃ | P2₁/n | 56.11 wt% |
| Cu₃WO₆ | Pa-3 | 34.47 wt% |
| CuO | C2/c | 9.42 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 4.4708%, Rp 3.0781%, GOF 1.8594. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Formal uncertainties are 0.12–0.17 wt%, though systematic uncertainty is likely around 1 wt%. Trace CuWO₄ cannot be excluded but was not supported reliably enough to include.

